WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:22.119
Hi I'm Toby and welcome back to another
episode of The Joy of Mathematics. I'm so

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:27.040
glad you could join me again today. Are
you ready to work on some mathematical

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.470
artwork? Because that's what I thought we would do today. No stress, no equations,

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.160
just relax
and gain an appreciation of our

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.809
mathematical world. I'm going to run some ideas across the bottom of the screen

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:45.190
that we will come to understand today. I
would also like to thank today's sponsor

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:53.520
brilliant.org. So I'm going to start off
my artwork by splitting it in two

00:00:53.520 --> 00:01:01.570
I'm going to have a line that separates
the sky from the grass. We're going to

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:10.000
get a beautiful blue sky by filling it
in like so, just some nice long strokes

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:25.570
there. All right, so in the sky don't
forget to add some sunlight and we can

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:36.130
also add some little birds in the corner
here. All right, so we're also going to

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:42.130
need to do the grass so grab a green and
just like we did the sky, fill in a nice

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:49.030
green healthy grass here. If you can see
the backdrop behind me today the grass

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:54.430
isn't so green, it's a little dry, it's
also a little smoky from some of the

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:59.010
smoke haze from bush fires we've got
going on :(

00:02:03.999 --> 00:02:10.670
All right, so we've set up our scene now
what I'm going to do is I'm going to

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:17.540
place a little mushroom right here in
the middle of the grass. It's the kind of

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:22.549
mushroom that would attract fairies, we
may be mathematicians today but

00:02:22.549 --> 00:02:27.830
we're not too serious that we can't
include a little bit of magic. So what I

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:34.549
want to do next is I want to draw a hill
that the mushroom is going to be on but

00:02:34.549 --> 00:02:39.349
just so that everything looks nice and
aesthetic I don't want the hill to be

00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:43.910
too close to the horizon and I don't
want it to be too close to the mushroom

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:49.340
either, in fact right in the middle of
these two would be perfect, and that's

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:53.810
what we're going to do. We're going to
measure out the distance here find

00:02:53.810 --> 00:03:00.859
halfway and that is going to be the tip
of our hill. I'm just going to switch us

00:03:00.859 --> 00:03:05.180
to our little chalk pen here so it's a
bit easier to see on the background of

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:10.730
the chalk. All right, so there's the tip
of our hill, now I thought about using a

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:14.840
ruler to do this, I even brought a ruler
along with me but then I thought at the

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.799
last minute that might be a bit of
overkill, we can just measure this using

00:03:18.799 --> 00:03:24.380
our minds and our brains, so if we have a
look here we want the hill to curve down,

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:29.269
we want it to be equally far away from
the horizon and the mushroom, so what

00:03:29.269 --> 00:03:33.859
would be equidistant? About there and
about there, I think that would be the

00:03:33.859 --> 00:03:37.910
next point of our hill and the same on
the other side if we measure with our

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:42.290
eyes down from here the same distance
from the horizon and the mushroom we

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:47.569
might get there. And continuing that
little measurement with our eyes all the

00:03:47.569 --> 00:03:56.560
way around we start to see what this
hill is going to look like

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:11.490
not everything needs to be done
perfectly with a ruler, I think maybe

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:16.739
that some of the fun of it is getting it
maybe slightly wrong but we know the

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:21.840
idea we're going for. I'm going to go
ahead and add some happy little trees to

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:33.960
our beautiful hill in here, not too many
we don't want to go overboard but these

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:51.540
are some happy little pine cones. That
one didn't quite make it to the hill.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:56.820
Pine cones are some of my favorite trees to draw. I've actually got a cone sitting up

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:01.860
here on my easel today to celebrate the
festive season, I considered wearing it

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.410
on my own head but I didn't want to mess
up my hair.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:09.990
You see cones are interesting shapes
because they contain other shapes inside

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:14.430
of them, let me show you what I mean. I've
got some scissors here and I'm going to

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:18.810
make a few cuts. So I think you can tell
that because of the shape of the cone if

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:24.120
I was to cut it straight across I would
get a circle but if I was to cut it a

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:29.310
little bit wonky across here it's a
little bit less obvious but I would get

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:41.700
an ellipse, a kind of stretched out
circle. Let's see if we can get one...

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:46.530
It's a little bit jagged but you can see
there I've cut on a wonky angle and I've

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:53.910
got an ellipse out and now what I'm
going to do is cut this one more way and

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:59.490
that is going to be parallel to one of
the sides. So this is the side that I

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:04.590
want to be parallel to, I'm going to make
a cut across here like this and we'll

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:09.440
see what shape we get so here we go...

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:21.130
So I've tried my best there to cut this
side parallel and this is the shape that

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:28.270
we've ended up with. It may shock you
to know but this shape here is actually

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:32.950
exactly the same shape as our hill that
we drew before. That's now two different

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:38.860
places that we've found this shape
hiding, let's see if I trace it out.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:43.960
This is a little precarious but that's the
fun of it, so I'm just tracing around the

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:50.550
shape that we cut out from our cone to
see what it looks like on our artwork.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:57.340
All right, moment of truth, there we go
yeah that looks about right, looks quite

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:03.130
similar to our hill. Now this shape has a
name and it is called a parabola.

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:07.360
Now we've found parabolas already in a
couple of places but they actually occur

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.340
everywhere in nature as well as in many
man-made things here's some footage of a

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:16.420
couple of other parabolas that I found
around town. There's a parabola in the

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:20.950
curve of some dishes at the physics
department and in the falling water of a

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:25.840
fountain near the library. A water
fountain or any object thrown up into

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:30.700
the air at an angle will trace out a
parabola, the object retains its

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:35.590
horizontal motion (if we neglect air
resistance) and so the only force acting

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:41.440
on it is gravity causing a downward
acceleration. Like many of nature's

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.330
secrets, humans have taken inspiration
from the parabolas. One useful property

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:51.580
that the parabolas have is that they
reflect all parallel light rays to a

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:54.240
focus.

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:10.170
A mushroom was the focus of our last
parabola in here but if this up here is

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:16.530
a big dish then we could put some
electronics here in the focus or in the

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:20.430
case of the one I showed you before from
the physics department you could maybe

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:25.680
heat up some water in here as a
demonstration of some solar heating.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:31.230
It works both ways, a torch and headlights
on a car would have a parabolic mirror

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:35.430
with the bulb in the middle. Light from
the bulb would be reflected off the

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:40.410
parabolic mirror out into a beam of
light. So let's just add a little bit

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:45.960
more color to our drawing here, you're
basically done we can add in a few more

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:55.620
parabolic hills, don't go overboard just
keep it nice and pretty. I hope that this

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:59.700
video has been a little bit of
inspiration for you to keep an eye out

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:06.090
for mathematical shapes all around you.
If you find a way to work some parabolas

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:12.200
into an artwork of your own then send it
to me and I'll share my favorite ones on Twitter.

